WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Scott Shawyer will return to competition in January, 2027, teaming up with Alan Roberts for the RORC Transatlantic Race on the foiling, IMOCA, IMO4. This is Offshore North Episode 11. One person is trying to become the first Canadian to sail alone, nonstop, around the world. On the Canadian boat, the team's YB tracker shows IMO4 alongside in Gospor, UK, Portsmouth Harbor as of October 26, 2026. Canada Ocean Racing announced on October 23 that Scott Shawyer will team up with British Offshore Sailor Alan Roberts for the RORC.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:34.940
C transatlantic race, which starts on January 10th, 2027 from Marina, Lanzarote.

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.460
Roberts finished fourth in the ocean race with bio-therm and race nine seasons on the French

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:40.600
Figaro solo circuit.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.740
In 2023, the pair won their class and set the race's double-handed course record, nine

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:50.640
days, 12 hours, and 26 minutes on the team's earlier IMO say, not IMO IV.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.580
This time, they will sail the 3,000 nautical mile crossing from Lanzarote to Antigua on

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:56.740
the foiling IMO IV.

00:00:56.740 --> 00:01:01.120
The post notes that Shire is looking ahead after the decision not to compete in the route

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:01.420
to rum.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:05.680
The team states that the crossing will allow him to get back on the water and build valuable

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.320
experience aboard Imerafor.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:13.220
The RORC transatlantic isn't a Vende qualifier, but it's valuable ocean time on Imerafor.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:18.920
Scott still needs a solo grade two finish, such as Retour Alibaz 2027 or the Transat

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.640
CIC 2028, plus a top 37 globe series ranking.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.760
The campaign's public goal remains the first Canadian finish in the 2028 edition, which

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.600
starts on November 12th, 2028.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:36.174
Now the fleet, the expert panel assembled by sailors for the 2026 Root Durum IMOSA class

00:01:36.174 --> 00:01:41.594
named Jeremy Bayou, the clear favorite, followed by Sam Goodchild and Embrogeo Becaria.

00:01:42.154 --> 00:01:44.934
Four of the six skippers pulled picked Bayou on Charaal.

00:01:45.394 --> 00:01:53.394
The panel included the last two Root Durum IMOSA winners, Paul Mailhat from 2018 and Thomas

00:01:53.394 --> 00:01:57.794
Ioann Rishom, Nicholas Lunven, Romain Atenasio, and Justine Metro.

00:01:58.414 --> 00:02:02.814
The panel noted a drop to 22 IMOC entries from the record 38 in 2022.

00:02:03.554 --> 00:02:07.794
Thomas Riyant attributed that drop to companies hesitating on long-term sponsorship commitments.

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:12.574
Riyant said the projects are built around long-term commitments, so as soon as confidence

00:02:12.574 --> 00:02:15.254
in the future starts to waver, the impact is felt immediately.

00:02:15.754 --> 00:02:20.094
He added that companies are finding it difficult to commit for the long-term, yet paradoxically

00:02:20.094 --> 00:02:22.674
enthusiasm for what the sailors do has not diminished.

00:02:23.154 --> 00:02:27.054
The panel agreed that around 10 skippers have a realistic chance of a podium finish or

00:02:27.054 --> 00:02:27.954
a top five result.

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:32.334
Nicholas Lunevin said he was blown away by Cheryl's performance in the Transat Cafe

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:36.494
lore, where the boat posted significantly higher average speeds than rivals downwind

00:02:36.494 --> 00:02:37.294
in the trade winds.

00:02:37.734 --> 00:02:41.114
Lunevin highlighted the team's work on the rudder system that helps lift the stern,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:44.654
calling it a clever solution that delivers a significant performance game.

00:02:45.314 --> 00:02:49.094
Thomas Ruyant added that Jeremy has come through a season in which he has continued

00:02:49.094 --> 00:02:52.754
to learn a lot about Shiral, whose hull underwent a major refit last winter.

00:02:53.254 --> 00:02:56.614
The reduced field still contains roughly 10 realistic podium contenders.

00:02:57.054 --> 00:03:01.154
The economic pressure described by Rheant affects every campaign's ability to keep boats

00:03:01.154 --> 00:03:03.834
competitive through the 2028 Vendée Globe cycle.

00:03:04.314 --> 00:03:08.654
A smaller entry list does not reduce the technical demands on the boats or the skippers who must

00:03:08.654 --> 00:03:10.354
manage them alone across the Atlantic.

00:03:11.427 --> 00:03:16.087
Skippers were presented to the public in St. Mallow on 26 October, ahead of the 1 November

00:03:16.087 --> 00:03:16.427
start.

00:03:16.427 --> 00:03:20.767
Violet Dorange received the strongest reception among the IMOC sailors.

00:03:21.347 --> 00:03:24.767
The two vintage classes kicked things off, followed by the Class 40s.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:29.547
On stage, each sailor was accompanied by a child clutching an origami paper boat carrying

00:03:29.547 --> 00:03:29.907
a dream.

00:03:30.547 --> 00:03:34.407
The sailors from St. Mallow in the Ocean 50 class seemed to have a clear advantage in

00:03:34.407 --> 00:03:34.787
the applause.

00:03:35.487 --> 00:03:40.367
An energized Alexia Barrier burst onto the stage to represent the ULTEAM class, escorted

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:41.547
by the eight other sailors.

00:03:42.147 --> 00:03:46.127
codgerly air said anything as possible on the root-do-rum. The event marks the

00:03:46.127 --> 00:03:50.307
final public gathering before the fleet sales. The visible enthusiasm shows how

00:03:50.307 --> 00:03:54.147
the root-do-rum functions as the sports largest single-race festival even when

00:03:54.147 --> 00:03:57.947
entry numbers are lower than in previous editions. The presentation sequence

00:03:57.947 --> 00:04:01.207
gives every class its moment in front of the crowd before the boats head out

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:06.218
across the Atlantic. Time for plain sailing. Double-handed offshore racing requires

00:04:06.218 --> 00:04:10.458
two people to share every task that a solo skipper must perform alone. In the

00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:14.978
The RORC transatlantic, the pair must keep the boat moving at all times, manage sail

00:04:14.978 --> 00:04:19.118
changes, repairs, and roading decisions, and still find time for sleep in short watches.

00:04:19.678 --> 00:04:21.158
The boat itself does not change.

00:04:21.578 --> 00:04:26.158
The same foiling IMO say that will race solo in the Vendeglobe must be handled by two instead

00:04:26.158 --> 00:04:26.498
of one.

00:04:26.958 --> 00:04:30.138
The key difference is that one person can always be on deck while the other rests.

00:04:30.678 --> 00:04:34.918
This format therefore serves as useful preparation for the longer solo races that count toward

00:04:34.918 --> 00:04:36.178
Vendeglobe qualification.

00:04:36.178 --> 00:04:41.478
The 3,000 nautical mile distance from Lenzarot to Antigua forces the pair to manage the

00:04:41.478 --> 00:04:45.978
same weather systems and boat systems that appear in solo transatlantic races, yet the

00:04:45.978 --> 00:04:46.898
workload is divided.

00:04:47.498 --> 00:04:51.518
Double-handed racing lets the team test those systems and that boat in real ocean conditions

00:04:51.518 --> 00:04:54.018
without the full solo burden, and the countdown.

00:04:55.594 --> 00:04:59.694
748 days remain until the Vende Globe start on November 12, 2028.

00:05:00.234 --> 00:05:04.754
The campaign's next concrete milestone is the RORC transatlantic race, starting on

00:05:04.754 --> 00:05:08.134
January 10th, 2027, from Marina, Lanzarot.

00:05:08.614 --> 00:05:09.934
That's your week in offshore racing.

